
update products set
  quick_answer = 'Melanotan 2 (CAS 121062-08-6) is a synthetic, nonselective melanocortin receptor agonist studied for its activity at MC1R, MC3R, and MC4R, supplied as a 20mg/10ml nasal spray strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research into pigmentation-pathway and receptor-signalling mechanisms. It is an unlicensed medicine in the UK, and UK regulators have previously issued public safety warnings about unlicensed “tanning injection” products sold online.',
  content_sections = '[{"heading": null, "body": "Melanotan 2 is one of the most searched-for research peptides in the UK market, and also one of the most inconsistently described. Many listings market it directly as a tanning product, implying a cosmetic benefit from use. <strong>Melanotan 2 Nasal Spray</strong> is sold on a different basis: as a research compound for studying melanocortin receptor activity, supplied with the same batch documentation and third-party testing standards as Synedica UK''s other research peptides. This page sets out the chemistry, what published research and case-report literature have found, and what UK regulators have said about unlicensed melanotan products — so researchers can make an informed decision before ordering."}, {"heading": "What Melanotan 2 Is", "body": "Melanotan 2 is a synthetic cyclic heptapeptide analogue of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH), with the sequence Ac-Nle-cyclo[Asp-His-D-Phe-Arg-Trp-Lys]-NH2. It carries CAS number 121062-08-6, molecular formula C50H69N15O9, and a molecular weight of approximately 1,024.2 g/mol.<br /><br />Unlike Melanotan 1 (afamelanotide), which is a selective MC1R agonist, Melanotan 2 is a nonselective melanocortin receptor agonist — it activates MC1R (the receptor most associated with pigmentation), MC3R, and MC4R. MC4R activation is also implicated in appetite and sexual-function signalling pathways, which is why Melanotan 2''s receptor profile differs meaningfully from Melanotan 1''s, and why research and case-report literature on the two peptides is not interchangeable."}, {"heading": "What Current Research Says", "body": "The original human pharmacology data comes from a 1996 pilot phase-I clinical study (Dorr et al., <em>Life Sciences</em>) that evaluated Melanotan-II as a melanotropic peptide and reported its receptor-agonism and pigmentation effects in a small clinical cohort — this is decades-old, small-scale clinical pharmacology data, not a modern safety or efficacy trial, and it does not describe an unlicensed, non-pharmaceutical-grade product purchased online.<br /><br />Because Melanotan 2 is nonselective across MC1R, MC3R, and MC4R, published case-report literature has documented adverse events linked to its receptor activity, including priapism (Devlin et al., 2013, <em>Clinical Toxicology</em>; Dreyer et al., 2019, <em>BMJ Case Reports</em>) and a case report of melanoma occurring in a melanotan user with subsequent pigmented-lesion changes (Paurobally et al., 2011, <em>British Journal of Dermatology</em>). These are case reports describing adverse events in individual patients following use of unregulated melanotan products, not a characterisation of this specific research-use listing, and they are not a substitute for a controlled trial — but they are directly relevant to why melanocortin receptor agonists of this kind remain an active subject of pharmacological research rather than an established cosmetic product."}, {"heading": "Regulatory and Safety Status in the UK", "body": "Melanotan 2 is not a controlled drug under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971. It is classified by the MHRA as a medicinal product within the meaning of the <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Human Medicines Regulations 2012 (SI 2012/1916)</a>, and no melanotan product holds a UK marketing authorisation. UK regulators have previously issued public safety warnings specifically about melanotan products marketed online as unlicensed “tanning injections”, citing concerns about unverified sourcing, inconsistent purity, and the adverse events described in the case-report literature above. This listing is supplied strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research use, is not sold as a cosmetic or tanning product, and is not licensed, assessed, or approved by the MHRA for any human use, application, or benefit."}, {"heading": "Specifications", "body": "<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Property</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 8px;\">Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">CAS number</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">121062-08-6</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Molecular formula</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">C50H69N15O9</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Molecular weight</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">≈ 1,024.2 g/mol</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Sequence</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Ac-Nle-cyclo[Asp-His-D-Phe-Arg-Trp-Lys]-NH2</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Receptor activity</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Nonselective agonist: MC1R, MC3R, MC4R</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Form supplied</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;\">Nasal spray, 20mg/10ml</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">UK legal / regulatory status</td><td style=\"padding:6px 8px;\">Unlicensed medicine under the Human Medicines Regulations 2012 — not a controlled drug, not MHRA-approved, supplied strictly for laboratory research</td></tr></tbody></table><br />The CAS number and molecular formula are independently verifiable against public chemical databases such as PubChem."}, {"heading": "Purity, Batch Testing and Certificate of Analysis Standards", "body": "Every <strong>Melanotan 2 Nasal Spray</strong> unit offered for sale is supplied with third-party analytical documentation confirming peptide purity, the analytical method used (typically HPLC or mass spectrometry), the batch or lot number, and the issuing laboratory. Purity testing verifies chemical identity and concentration; it does not address the receptor-mediated effects described in the case-report literature above, which relate to the compound''s pharmacology rather than its purity. See our <a href=\"/contact\">contact page</a> to request a Certificate of Analysis for a specific batch."}, {"heading": "Sourcing and Verification", "body": "Because Melanotan 2 is widely marketed online outside a research-use framing, verifying a supplier matters more than usual. Before ordering, check that the listing states research-use status clearly rather than implying a cosmetic or tanning benefit, that batch documentation is offered rather than promised only “on request,” and that the compound''s identity (CAS number, molecular formula) is independently verifiable against a source such as PubChem. <strong>Melanotan 2 Nasal Spray</strong> is offered for sale on this basis, with documentation available per batch. Researchers examining other cosmetic-adjacent research peptides may also find the <a href=\"/product/glow-skin-112mg\">Glow Skin 112mg blend</a> relevant, and the full catalogue is browsable on the <a href=\"/products\">research products page</a>."}]'::jsonb,
  faq = '[{"question": "What is Melanotan 2 used for?", "answer": "It is supplied for laboratory and in-vitro research into melanocortin receptor activity and pigmentation-pathway signalling. It is not sold as a tanning product, a cosmetic product, or for any therapeutic, dietary, or performance use."}, {"question": "What is the difference between Melanotan 1 and Melanotan 2?", "answer": "Melanotan 1 (afamelanotide) is a selective MC1R agonist. Melanotan 2 is a nonselective agonist that activates MC1R, MC3R, and MC4R. This broader receptor activity is why the two peptides have different research and case-report profiles and are not interchangeable."}, {"question": "Is Melanotan 2 legal to buy in the UK?", "answer": "Melanotan 2 is not a controlled drug under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1971. It is classified by the MHRA as an unlicensed medicine under the Human Medicines Regulations 2012 (SI 2012/1916), and no melanotan product holds a UK marketing authorisation. It is supplied here strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research use, not for human application, and has not been assessed by the MHRA for safety or efficacy."}, {"question": "Has the MHRA issued any warnings about melanotan products?", "answer": "UK regulators have previously issued public safety warnings about melanotan products marketed online as unlicensed “tanning injections,” citing concerns about unverified sourcing and adverse events. This listing is not sold or marketed as a tanning product."}, {"question": "What adverse events have been reported in connection with Melanotan 2?", "answer": "Peer-reviewed case reports have documented priapism (Devlin et al., 2013, Clinical Toxicology; Dreyer et al., 2019, BMJ Case Reports) and a case of melanoma with pigmented-lesion changes in a melanotan user (Paurobally et al., 2011, British Journal of Dermatology). These describe adverse events in individual patients following use of unregulated products, not a characterisation of this research-use listing."}, {"question": "Why would Melanotan 2 be linked to priapism in case reports?", "answer": "Melanotan 2 is a nonselective melanocortin receptor agonist that activates MC4R alongside MC1R and MC3R. MC4R activity has an established link to sexual-function signalling pathways in the pharmacology literature, which is the mechanistic basis referenced in the priapism case reports above."}, {"question": "What does the Certificate of Analysis (COA) show for this product?", "answer": "A COA confirms peptide purity, the analytical testing method used (typically HPLC or mass spectrometry), the batch or lot number, and the issuing laboratory. It verifies chemical identity and concentration, not the receptor-mediated effects described in the case-report literature."}, {"question": "How is the CAS number used to verify this compound?", "answer": "CAS 121062-08-6 identifies Melanotan 2. Researchers can cross-check this number, along with the molecular formula C50H69N15O9, against an independent database such as PubChem to confirm chemical identity before use."}, {"question": "Does the nasal spray format affect the research application?", "answer": "The nasal spray is a delivery/packaging format only. This listing makes no dosing, administration, or usage recommendations; researchers determine their own protocols in line with their institution''s research and safety governance."}, {"question": "How should Melanotan 2 Nasal Spray be stored before use?", "answer": "Store according to the conditions printed on the batch documentation and product label. Exact storage parameters and shelf life are batch-specific and confirmed on the accompanying documentation."}, {"question": "Can this product be used for tanning, cosmetic, human, or veterinary applications?", "answer": "No. It is sold strictly for laboratory and in-vitro research use. It is not a cosmetic or tanning product, is not for human or veterinary use or application, is not licensed under the Human Medicines Regulations 2012, and has not been assessed by the MHRA for safety or efficacy under this listing."}, {"question": "Where can I buy Melanotan 2 for research use?", "answer": "It is available for sale directly from Synedica UK, supplied with batch documentation and third-party testing for verified UK research buyers."}]'::jsonb,
  author_line = 'Synedica UK Science & QA Team',
